WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.737
Server-side enforcement begins by
establishing who or what is making the request.

2
00:00:04.817 --> 00:00:08.872
Authentication middleware should verify
the session or token according to the

3
00:00:08.952 --> 00:00:12.100
application's protocol before a privileged
operation runs.

4
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.569
Verification includes more than decoding a
token-shaped string.

5
00:00:15.649 --> 00:00:18.796
The server must validate the signature or
session reference,

6
00:00:18.876 --> 00:00:22.024
expected issuer and audience where
applicable, expiration,

7
00:00:22.104 --> 00:00:25.372
and any revocation or session-state rules
the system relies on.

8
00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:28.479
The result should be a stable internal
subject identifier,

9
00:00:28.559 --> 00:00:32.372
not a display name or an arbitrary
identifier copied from the request body.

10
00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:36.265
From that verified identity, the server
builds a trusted request context.

11
00:00:36.345 --> 00:00:39.977
A TypeScript interface can represent
fields such as user ID, tenant ID,

12
00:00:40.057 --> 00:00:44.051
and roles, but those values must originate
from trusted server-side sources.

13
00:00:44.131 --> 00:00:48.489
Tenant membership, permissions, and
feature entitlements are usually loaded from a

14
00:00:48.569 --> 00:00:52.988
session record, identity provider mapping,
or authoritative application database.

15
00:00:53.068 --> 00:00:54.702
A body containing userId, role,

16
00:00:54.782 --> 00:00:57.324
or tenantId may help describe a requested
target,

17
00:00:57.404 --> 00:01:01.520
but it cannot grant identity or privilege
merely because the client supplied it.

18
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.837
A small AuthContext type gives route
handlers and services a consistent contract.

19
00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:09.730
Authentication middleware can attach a
validated context to the request,

20
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.715
while downstream code accepts that context
explicitly.

21
00:01:12.795 --> 00:01:16.972
This is preferable to repeatedly
interpreting headers or body properties in each

22
00:01:17.052 --> 00:01:17.536
handler.

23
00:01:17.616 --> 00:01:21.853
TypeScript then helps prevent accidental
omissions and inconsistent field names

24
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:23.022
across the codebase.

25
00:01:23.102 --> 00:01:25.826
It does not verify the runtime origin of
those fields,

26
00:01:25.906 --> 00:01:29.961
so the boundary that constructs
AuthContext must remain narrow and auditable.

27
00:01:30.041 --> 00:01:33.733
Authentication and authorization remain
separate responsibilities.

28
00:01:33.813 --> 00:01:36.053
A valid session establishes the requester;

29
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:40.007
it does not prove access to every tenant,
record, or administrative action.

30
00:01:40.087 --> 00:01:44.324
Downstream policy still needs the trusted
subject plus the requested resource and

31
00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:45.251
relevant state.

32
00:01:45.331 --> 00:01:49.386
This pattern works whether the transport
layer uses Express, Fastify, NestJS,

33
00:01:49.466 --> 00:01:52.008
or a custom HTTP server: verify
credentials once,

34
00:01:52.088 --> 00:01:54.267
construct trusted context deliberately,

35
00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:58.100
and pass that context to the code
responsible for each security decision.
